% Generated by hand from roxygen2 comments in R/nutrigon_curvature.R
\name{nutrigon_curvature}
\alias{nutrigon_curvature}
\title{Local Gaussian and mean curvature, and surface-area ratio, of a fitted
surface}
\usage{
nutrigon_curvature(fit, n = 50, quiet = FALSE)
}
\arguments{
\item{fit}{A \code{"nutrigon_fit"} object (from \code{\link{nutrigon_fit}}).}

\item{n}{Grid resolution per axis (default 50, matching the resolution
used in the original curvature analysis this function is based on).
Only points inside the convex hull of the design are returned.}

\item{quiet}{Suppress the link-scale reminder message for GLM fits
(default \code{FALSE}).}
}
\value{
A list with components:
\describe{
  \item{grid}{A data frame with columns named after the original
    \code{x_col} and \code{y_col}, plus \code{Gauss_curv} and
    \code{Mean_curv}, one row per grid point inside the design hull.}
  \item{surface_area_ratio}{A single number: the fitted surface's area
    divided by a flat reference surface's area over the same design
    bounding box (see Details). Requires \pkg{pracma}.}
}
}
\description{
Computes the local Gaussian curvature and mean curvature of a fitted
quadratic surface at every point of a grid restricted to the convex hull
of the design, together with a surface-area ratio (the fitted surface's
area relative to a flat surface spanning the same design). This treats
the fitted response surface as a 2-manifold embedded in 3-space (the
first and second fundamental forms of differential geometry), which is a
different notion of "curvature" from the Hessian-eigenvalue elongation
ratio returned by \code{\link{nutrigon_elongation}} -- elongation
describes the \emph{shape} of the quadratic (how stretched the
peak/valley is along its principal axes), while Gaussian/mean curvature
describe the \emph{local bending} of the surface itself at each point.
}
\details{
For \code{model_type = "glm"} fits (see \code{\link{nutrigon_fit}}),
curvature is computed on the \strong{link scale}, consistent with how the
optimum itself is located and how \code{\link{nutrigon_elongation}}
computes elongation. The fitted quadratic's coefficients are read via the
same internal dispatch layer used throughout the package, so curvature is
available identically for \code{"lm"}, \code{"glm"}, and \code{"lmer"}
fits.

Gaussian curvature (\code{Gauss_curv}) is the product of the two
principal curvatures at a point; mean curvature (\code{Mean_curv}) is
their average. Both are computed in closed form from the first and
second fundamental forms of the fitted quadratic surface, following
standard differential geometry (see e.g. do Carmo, \emph{Differential
Geometry of Curves and Surfaces}).

The surface-area ratio (\code{surface_area_ratio}, a single number
returned alongside the grid, not per-point) is the fitted surface's area
over the design hull, divided by the area of a flat surface spanning the
same hull. A ratio of 1 indicates a flat response; larger values indicate
a more strongly curved (bowl-, dome-, or saddle-shaped) surface. This is
computed by numerically integrating the surface-area element (via
\pkg{pracma}) over the bounding box of the design, for both the fitted
surface and a flat reference surface fitted to a constant response over
the same design.
}
\examples{
data(lee_drosophila)
fit_lifespan <- nutrigon_fit(lee_drosophila, "protein_eaten", "carb_eaten",
                              "lifespan", design_id_col = "treatment")
curv_lifespan <- nutrigon_curvature(fit_lifespan, n = 30)
head(curv_lifespan$grid)
curv_lifespan$surface_area_ratio

}
\seealso{
\code{\link{nutrigon_fit}}, \code{\link{nutrigon_elongation}},
\code{\link{nutrigon_grid}}
}
